\section*{Explicit Form of the Second Pfaffian Compound $\mathcal{P}_2(A)$
for a $4 \times 4$ Skew-Symmetric Matrix $A$}

Consider a general $4\times 4$ skew-symmetric matrix: 
\begin{equation*}
A=%
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & a_{12} & a_{13} & a_{14} \\ 
-a_{12} & 0 & a_{23} & a_{24} \\ 
-a_{13} & -a_{23} & 0 & a_{34} \\ 
-a_{14} & -a_{24} & -a_{34} & 0%
\end{pmatrix}%
.
\end{equation*}

The \textbf{second Pfaffian compound matrix} $\mathcal{P}_2(A)$ is
constructed from the Pfaffians of all $2 \times 2$ principal submatrices of $%
A$.

\subsection*{Step 1: Identify the $2 \times 2$ Principal Submatrices}

There are $\binom{4}{2} = 6$ principal submatrices. Their Pfaffians are: 
\begin{align*}
\text{Pf}(A_{\{1,2\}}) &= a_{12}, \\
\text{Pf}(A_{\{1,3\}}) &= a_{13}, \\
\text{Pf}(A_{\{1,4\}}) &= a_{14}, \\
\text{Pf}(A_{\{2,3\}}) &= a_{23}, \\
\text{Pf}(A_{\{2,4\}}) &= a_{24}, \\
\text{Pf}(A_{\{3,4\}}) &= a_{34}.
\end{align*}

\subsection*{Step 2: Construct $\mathcal{P}_2(A)$ as a Skew-Symmetric Matrix}

The compound matrix $\mathcal{P}_2(A)$ is a $6 \times 6$ skew-symmetric
matrix. Its entries are determined by: 
\begin{equation*}
(\mathcal{P}_2(A))_{I,J} = \text{Pf}(A_{I \cup J}) \cdot \text{sgn}(I, J), 
\end{equation*}
where $\text{sgn}(I,J)$ is the permutation sign for merging $I$ and $J$.

\subsection*{Step 3: Explicit Form of $\mathcal{P}_2(A)$}

Ordering rows/columns lexicographically as $\{1,2\}, \{1,3\}, \{1,4\},
\{2,3\}, \{2,4\}, \{3,4\}$, we obtain: 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{P}_2(A) = 
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & a_{23} & -a_{24} & -a_{13} & a_{14} & 0 \\ 
- a_{23} & 0 & a_{34} & a_{12} & 0 & -a_{14} \\ 
a_{24} & -a_{34} & 0 & 0 & -a_{12} & a_{13} \\ 
a_{13} & -a_{12} & 0 & 0 & a_{34} & -a_{24} \\ 
- a_{14} & 0 & a_{12} & -a_{34} & 0 & a_{23} \\ 
0 & a_{14} & -a_{13} & a_{24} & -a_{23} & 0 \\ 
&  &  &  &  & 
\end{pmatrix}%
. 
\end{equation*}

\subsection*{Verification}

The Pfaffian identity holds: 
\begin{equation*}
\text{Pf}(\mathcal{P}_2(A)) = \text{Pf}(A)^{\binom{3}{1}} = \text{Pf}(A)^3. 
\end{equation*}

\subsection*{Remarks}

\begin{itemize}
\item For larger matrices, $\mathcal{P}_{2k}(A)$ follows analogous but more
complex constructions. 

\item Applications include fermionic systems in physics and algebraic
combinatorics.
\end{itemize}

